<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 3.5 - Subspaces of <m>\real^p</m></title>

    <introduction>
        <definition xml:id="def-3-5-subspace">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>subspace</term> of <m>\mathbb{R}^p</m> is a subset
                    of <m>\mathbb{R}^p</m> that is the span of a set of vectors.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            In this section we will look at some important subspaces.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's start by understanding a few things about subspaces.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \dots, \vvec_n</m> is a set of
                    vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>, explain why the zero vector
                    <m>{\bf 0}</m> can be expressed as a linear combination of
                    these vectors.
                    Usse this fact to explain why <m>{\bf 0}</m> belongs to any
                    subspace in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the line on the left is not a subspace of
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, and the line on the right is.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="figure-lines-r2">
                    <caption>
                        Two lines in <m>\real^2</m>, one of which is a subspace
                        and one of which is not.
                    </caption>

                    <sidebyside widths="40% 40%" margins="5% 5%">
                        <image>
                            <shortdescription>
                                A line in two dimensions that does not pass
                                through the origin.
                            </shortdescription>

                            <description>
                                <p>
                                    A line in two dimensions that does not pass
                                    through the origin.
                                </p>
                            </description>
                            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-subspace-line-skew">
                                <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
                                    <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                                        <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                                        <line at="line" endpoints="((0,0),(2,1))" stroke="blue" infinite="yes"/>
                                    </coordinates>
                                    <annotations text="A line passing through negative two, negative one and two one">
                                        <annotation ref="line" text="The line"/>
                                    </annotations>
                                </diagram>
                            </prefigure>
                        </image>

                        <image>
                            <shortdescription>
                                A line in two dimensions that does passes
                                through the origin.
                            </shortdescription>

                            <description>
                                <p>
                                    A line in two dimensions that does passes
                                    through the origin.
                                </p>
                            </description>
                            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-subspace-line-origin">
                                <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
                                    <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                                        <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                                        <line at="line" endpoints="((0,0),(2,1))" stroke="blue" infinite="yes"/>
                                    </coordinates>
                                    <annotations text="A line passing through negative two, negative one and two one">
                                        <annotation ref="line" text="The line"/>
                                    </annotations>
                                </diagram>
                            </prefigure>
                        </image>
                    </sidebyside>
                </figure>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, <m>\vvec_3</m>,
                    and <m>\vvec_4</m> are four vectors in <m>\real^3</m> and
                    that
                    <md>
                        \begin{bmatrix}
		  \vvec_1 \amp \vvec_2 \amp \vvec_3 \amp \vvec_4
		  \end{bmatrix}
		  \sim
		  \begin{bmatrix}
		  1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\
		  0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
		  0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
		  \end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                    Give a description of the subspace
                    <m>S=\laspan{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4}</m> of
                    <m>\real^3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        The first important subspace we'll look at is called the <term>column space</term>
        of <m>A</m> and denoted as <m>\col(A)</m>.
        It's just the span of the columns of <m>A</m>.
    </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-3-5-colspace">
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix we call the span of its colums the <term>column space</term> of <m>A</m> and denote it as <m>col(A)</m>. That is 
              <md>
            \col(A) = \laspan{\vvec_1, \vvec_2,
  \vvec_3, \vvec_4}.
        </md>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </definition>
        

    <exercise xml:id="exer-3-5-col">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the matrix
                <md>
                    A = \begin{bmatrix}
  \vvec_1 \amp \vvec_2 \amp \vvec_3 \amp \vvec_4
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
  2 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
  -2 \amp 2 \amp -4 \amp -2 \\
\end{bmatrix}
\sim
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
  0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
  0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
\end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Give an example of a vector in <m>\col(A)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Is <m>\bvec = \threevec{15}{9}{-12}</m> in <m>\col(A)</m>? Is <m>\cvec = \fourvec1111</m> in <m>\col(A)</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Fill in the blanks: <m>\col(A)</m> is a subspace of <fillin characters="10" /> because <fillin characters="40" />.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are the vectors <m>\vvec_1,\ldots,\vvec_4</m> linearly
                    independent or dependent?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write <m>\vvec_3</m> and <m>\vvec_4</m> as a linear
                    combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why
                    <m>\col(A) = \laspan{\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3, \vvec_4} =
  \laspan{\vvec_1, \vvec_2}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m> form a
                    linearly independent set of vectors.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
             Describe <m>\col(A)</m> geometrically.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Since <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m> span <m>\col(A)</m>
                    and are linearly independent, we say they form a <term> basis </term> for
                    <m>\col(A)</m>.
                    The <term>dimension</term> of a subspace <m>S</m> is
                    denoted <m>\dim S</m> and is the number of vectors in a
                    basis for that subspace.
                    <m>\col(A)</m> is a subspace of <m>\real^3</m>- What is the
                    dimension of <m>\col(A)</m> (denoted <m>\dim \col(A)</m> )?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <definition xml:id="def-3-5-basisdim">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>basis</term> for a subspace <m>S</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^p</m> is a set of vectors in <m>S</m> that are linearly independent and whose span is <m>S</m>. We say that the <term>dimension</term> of the subspace <m>S</m>, denoted <m>\dim S</m> is the number of vectors in any basis.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-3-5-colB">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose we have a matrix
                <md>
                    B = \begin{bmatrix}
    \bvec_1 \amp \bvec_2 \amp \bvec_3 \amp \bvec_4 \amp \bvec_5
  \end{bmatrix}
  \sim
  \begin{bmatrix}
    1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
    0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
    0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
    0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
  \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace=".7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the shape of <m>B</m>; that is, how many rows and
                    columns does it have?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace=".7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>\laspan{\bvec_1} = \laspan{\fourvec1000}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In this case, the column space
                    <m>\col(B) = \laspan{\bvec_1, \bvec_2,
    \bvec_3, \bvec_4, \bvec_5}</m>.
                    Are these five vectors linearly independent?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How can we form a basis for <m>\col(B)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Fill in the blanks: <m>\col(B)</m> is a
                   <fillin characters="5" />-dimensional subspace of <m>\real^p</m> where
                    <m>p=</m> <fillin characters="5" />
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Generally speaking, what is the relationship between the
                dimension <m>\col(C)</m> and the number of pivots in <m>C</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

              <p>
                The second subspace we'll look at is called the
                <term> null
    space</term> of <m>A</m> and is denoted
                <m>\nul(A)</m>.
                It's just the solution set of the equation
                <m>A\xvec = \zerovec</m>.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-3-5-null">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  If <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix we call the subset of vectors <m>\xvec</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> satisfying <m>A\xvec={\bf 0}</m> the <term>null space</term> of <m>A</m> and denote it by <m>\nul(A)</m>.
                </p>
              </statement>
            </definition>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's now go back to the matrix <m>A</m> from <xref ref="exer-3-5-col"/>, rewritten here:
                <md>
                    A = \begin{bmatrix}
  \vvec_1 \amp \vvec_2 \amp \vvec_3 \amp \vvec_4
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
  2 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
  -2 \amp 2 \amp -4 \amp -2 \\
\end{bmatrix}
\sim
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
  0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
  0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
\end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write the solution set to the equation
                    <m>A\xvec = \zerovec</m> as a parametric description.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use this parametric description to find two vectors
                    <m>\wvec_1</m> and <m>\wvec_2</m> for which
                    <m>\nul(A) = \laspan{\wvec_1,\wvec_2}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why <m>\wvec_1</m> and <m>\wvec_2</m> form a
                    linearly independent set of vectors.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do <m>\wvec_1</m> and <m>\wvec_2</m> form a basis for
                    <m>\nul(A)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In this case, since vectors in <m>\nul(A)</m> are in
                    <m>\real^4</m>, <m>\nul(A)</m> is a subspace of
                    <m>\real^4</m>.
                    What is the dimension of <m>\nul(A)</m>, that is what is
                    <m>\dim \nul(A)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's go back to the matrix <m>B</m> from <xref ref="exer-3-5-colB"/>, rewritten below
                <md>
                    B = \begin{bmatrix}
    \bvec_1 \amp \bvec_2 \amp \bvec_3 \amp \bvec_4 \amp \bvec_5
  \end{bmatrix}
  \sim
  \begin{bmatrix}
    1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
    0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
    0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
    0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
  \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a basis for <m>\nul(B)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Complete the sentence: <m>\nul(B)</m> is a
                    <fillin characters="5" />-dimensional subspace of <m>\real^p</m> where
                    <m>p=</m> <fillin characters="5" />.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>C</m> is a <m>50\times 100</m> matrix with 30
                pivots.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\col(C)</m> is a <fillin characters="5" />-dimensional subspace of
                    <m>\real^p</m> where <m>p=</m> <fillin characters="5" />.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\nul(C)</m> is a <fillin characters="5" />-dimensional subspace of
                    <m>\real^p</m> where <m>p=</m> <fillin characters="5" />.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <definition xml:id="def-3-5-rank">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>rank</term> of a matrix <m>A</m> is the number of pivot positions in <m>A</m> and is denoted by <m>\rank(A)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's make further connections between topics and new vocabulary.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace=".5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              How can we use information about the pivots to get a basis for <m>\col(C)</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace=".3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the relationship between the rank of a matrix
                    <m>A</m> and the dimension of <m>\col(A)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace=".5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>A</m> is an invertible <m>9\times 9</m> matrix, what can you say about <m>\col(A)</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Suppose that <m>A</m> is an <m>8\times 10</m> matrix and <m>\col(A)=\mathbb{R}^8</m>. If <m>\bvec</m> is an 8-dimensional vector, what can you say about the equation <m>A\xvec=\bvec</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Say <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix.
                    Fill in the blank:
                    <md>
                        \dim \nul(A) + \dim \col(A) = \underline{\hspace{.5in}}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions:
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the definition of a subspace?
                    How can we tell if something is a subspace?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the dimension of a subspace?
                    How do we find it?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we find the null space of a matrix?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we find the column space of a matrix?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the relationship between the dimension of the column
                    space and the dimension of the null space?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>